.TH CHMAP_CONSTRUCT_FULL_SCOPED 3 "2026-09-20" "c_collections" "C Collections Library - Macros"
.SH NAME
chmap_construct_full_scoped \- declare and create a hash map with custom allocation, hashing and key equality, destroyed on scope exit
.SH LIBRARY
C Collections (libccollections, \-lccollections)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include <ccollections/chashmap.h>

chmap_construct_full_scoped(hm_name, key_t, val_t, mmgmt_procs,
                            custom_hashing_proc,
                            custom_key_equality_proc);
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.nf
Declares a hash map handle named hm_name. It also declares the companion
type variables. These variables carry key_t and val_t to every later
type-inferred macro. The macro then creates a map that takes its memory from
mmgmt_procs, hashes its keys with custom_hashing_proc and compares them with
custom_key_equality_proc. It also attaches the cleanup attribute. This
attribute destroys the map at every exit from the enclosing scope, an early
return included.

This macro is chmap_construct_full(3) plus that cleanup attribute. It is
the widest of the scoped construct macros. chmap_construct_scoped(3) is
this macro with the default allocator and the map's own hashing.
chmap_construct_mp_scoped(3) gives only an allocator.
chmap_construct_ch_scoped(3) gives only a hashing procedure. Each of the
three arguments can be NULL, which selects the matching default. This one
macro therefore covers every combination.

The map picks its implementation strategy from key_t and val_t, as
chmap_create_full(3) describes. A custom hashing procedure does not change
that choice. A custom key equality procedure selects separate chaining.
.fi
.SH PARAMETERS
.nf
hm_name              Name for the map handle to declare
key_t                Key type; it tells every later macro how to read a key
                     and, with val_t, which implementation strategy the map
                     uses
val_t                Value type
mmgmt_procs          Pointer to a ccol_memmgmt_procs_t, or NULL for the
                     default allocator
custom_hashing_proc  A ccol_hashing_proc_t, or NULL to use the map's own
                     hashing for key_t
custom_key_equality_proc
                     A ccol_key_equality_proc_t, or NULL to use the map's
                     own key comparison
.fi
.SH RETURN VALUE
.nf
None. This macro declares variables and does not expand to an expression.
.fi
.SH "CUSTOM HASHING"
.nf
A custom procedure gets a pointer to the key bytes and their length. It
returns an unsigned long. It must return the same value for two keys that
the map considers equal. For a char * key, equal means equal contents and
not an equal pointer.

The return value does not need to avalanche. The map finalizes the value
that a custom procedure returns with its own bit-mixing step. It does this
before it reduces the value to a slot or a bucket. A procedure that puts
all of its entropy in the low bits therefore still spreads across the
table. The contract is only "any function of the key that respects the
map's notion of key equality".
.fi
.SH "CUSTOM KEY EQUALITY"
.nf
custom_key_equality_proc gets both keys and both sizes, and returns true
when the two are the same key. The map uses it in place of its built-in
comparison. It must agree with custom_hashing_proc: two keys that it
reports as equal must get the same hash. A key equality procedure
without a hashing procedure makes the creation fail, and this macro then
stops the program. A struct key with padding bytes needs both
procedures; see chmap_create_full(3).
.fi
.SH NOTES
.nf
Stops the program through ccol_fatal_err() if the creation fails. Use
chmap_declare_scoped(3) with chmap_init_full(3) when the caller must
handle a failure
Needs GCC or Clang, because it uses the cleanup attribute
All four members of ccol_memmgmt_procs_t must be non-NULL when the caller
gives one. The macro copies the procedures into the map. Those procedures
and the hashing and key equality procedures must stay callable for the
whole life of the map, which here ends at the closing brace of the
enclosing scope
Uses CCOL_DEFAULT_INITIAL_BUCKET_ARRAY_SIZE (16) buckets
Scope exit destroys the map and clears the handle. An explicit
chmap_destroy(3) earlier in the scope therefore stays correct
The companion type variables follow the scope of the handle. A function
that gets the handle therefore needs chmap_redeclare(3) there
.fi
.SH WARNINGS
.nf
The handle must not outlive its scope. Do not return the handle. Do not
store it somewhere that outlives the scope. Both of these hand out a handle
whose map is freed at the exit from the scope.
.fi
.SH EXAMPLES
.nf
ccol_memmgmt_procs_t procs = {
    .malloc = my_malloc, .calloc = my_calloc,
    .realloc = my_realloc, .free = my_free,
};
unsigned long hash_bytes(const void *p, size_t size);

void tally(char **words, size_t n) {
    chmap_construct_full_scoped(seen, char *, int, &procs, hash_bytes,
                                NULL);
    int one = 1;
    for (size_t i = 0; i < n; i++) {
        chmap_insert(seen, words[i], one);
    }
}                                     /* seen is destroyed here */
.fi
